        <!-- FAQ -->
        <section id="faq" class="ed-faq scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
          <details open>
            <summary><h3>Is the Sole F89 discontinued?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              It has been removed from Sole’s own stores. On 14 October 2026 it was not among the eight treadmills on Sole’s US site, its product page returned not found, and Sole’s Canadian and UK sites did not list it. We found no statement from Sole confirming discontinuation or naming a replacement, so ask Sole directly if it matters to you.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>What replaced the Sole F89?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Sole has not named a direct replacement. Its top folding model on the US store is now the F85, with a 22 by 60-inch deck and 15.6-inch screen, and the new F83 also has a 22-inch deck. Neither has the F89’s 21.5-inch screen, and Sole’s current listings show no decline on either.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>What is the difference between the Sole F85 and F89?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Sole’s own comparison described the same core: a 4.0 HP motor, 22 by 60-inch deck, 12 mph, incline and decline and a 375 lb rating. The F89 added a 21.5-inch Android touchscreen and an aluminium frame. Those F85 figures describe the earlier F85; Sole’s current F85 listing gives 3.5 HP, 350 lb and no decline.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>How much does the Sole F89 weigh?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Sole’s assembly guide gives 314 lb assembled and 351 lb in the box; Sole’s parent company’s product page and one independent spec table give 319 lb. Either way it is one of the heaviest folding treadmills for home use, and moving it safely takes at least two people and a dolly.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Does the Sole F89 need a subscription?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              No. The console runs Sole’s free SOLE+ app and its built-in programs without any membership. Zwift and Kinomap are supported over Bluetooth if you choose to pay for them, and streaming apps can be used with your own accounts. Nothing on the treadmill itself stops working without a subscription.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Is a used Sole F89 worth buying?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              It can be, at a fair discount, if you test it running through its full incline and decline range, confirm the touchscreen boots and connects, and can move a machine of over 300 lb. Ask whether any warranty remains and whether it transfers. If the price is close to a new current Sole model, buy the new one.
            </p>
          </details>
